e truth of the  matter is it's been rather difficult a  number of the major dailies that that I  attempted to talk to basically said  there's nothing new to talk about on  Salladhor we're not interested in an  interview I've gotten pretty good  coverage when I've gotten in the door  and and been able to talk to some and  then very pleased by that I think that  the media coverage of El Salvador is  very interesting the recent that is the  july/august issue of Nakhla has some  very good examples of what happens  there's a picture in Nicaragua of  children mothers waiting in line with  pails a caption that appeared with that  picture in Business Week was disgruntled  Nicaraguans waiting in long  and for ration food the photographer who  took that picture explained that that  was women and children getting free meat  and milk rations which are given to the  poor in Nicaragua today so we have those  kinds of distortions and about 80  percent of what Americans here it seems  like either comes from the  administration from the State Department  from the embassy those are the sources  that our media usually picks up and  listens to and it's very difficult to  hear the other side of the story when  you're on say talk shows like here in  Austin how did the caller's the people  who call in whether their attitudes well  I was very very surprised here in Austin  that about 80 percent of the calls have  been very supportive to the message that  I carry and that is that that we could  be seeking peaceful solutions in El  Salvador that perhaps we're stopping  change and adding to the violence rather  than understanding the processes  happening there that's not always always  true of course there is a lot of  red-baiting sometimes on talk shows I do  nighttime talk radio shows because the  audience is very different when I give a  public talk like I will here at the  University there may be 300 people  interested in Central America when I do  a talk radio show there may be 5,000 or 