r the Academy and I think in  some ways there were some seeds planted  there and what your was it that was 1967  60 and a little bit of 68 and I recall  vividly passing by those long lines of  people in silent vigils thinking that  they're a little bit confused I'm going  to Vietnam soon to defend the right to  protest but that's what America is all  about  and I never really had any dialogue with  people there but when I got to Vietnam I  slowly began to see things that bothered  me now this was at a time before  Watergate so for me to come to terms  with the fact that the president was  knowingly lying to the American public  was very difficult and I remember one  time Nixon coming on TV and telling the  American public there were no troops and  Louis nor did he intend to commit any I  knew that day that my classmates were  flying out of unmarked airplanes and  secret air bases resupply by America  c-130s so I began to come to grips with  those contradictions I looked around me  one time and my job was flying personnel  around a cargo of medical evacuation I  was a c-130 pilot and found in the back  of the aircraft that the majority people  were black Chicano or poor whites that  that the kinds of people that had been  around the UCLA for instance weren't  there and I recalled how everyone was  was busily trying to get a deferment was  trying to get into graduate school or to  get married or have a child or whatever  it took there was this whole series of  things that had been very foreign to me  and I saw the effect of that because the  kids I was I was taking out to those  fire bases were for the most part there  because they had no alternatives they  had no choices that other people had  what was the bang for their attitudes  were they looking forward to they want  to go kill him some gooks or were they  resentful or were they did they have any  awareness once they were scared they  were scared to talk to me because I was  an officer and even engaging in the  conversation was difficult and it was  obvious to me that these were people who  who wouldn't challenge Authority very  easy for white middle-class people to  challenge authority  when their fathers maybe hired a lawyer  for them or tried to get them a  psychiatrist to help them get out of  things but these were kids from from  rural Kansas for instance who who never  would have challenged the authority of a  draft board and what were your actual  activities yourself in Vietnam were you  doing on flying missions were you in  combat I was flying water term combat  missions but that means just flying in  any area that was contested at some  point I had decided I didn't want to  kill anybody so I was a noncombatant I  flew a c-130 my missions really covered  most of the theater I flew into Cambodia  I flew in the Thailand into Vietnam and  many other parts of Southeast Asia and  it afforded me much more than the  bird's-eye view of the war that most  soldiers had who were just trying to  preserve their physical integrity I  didn't have a very dangerous job and in  the sense that others did did you see a  lot of ecological damage and a lot of  defoliation and destruction environment  from the bombing oh absolutely I  remember looking at at areas of Laos and  areas of Vietnam but I recall more  vividly Laos flying over the the Ho Chi  Minh Trail that looked like the moon I  mean just knew acres of pockmarked  fields that were without vegetation and  there was much of the countryside that  was just completely destroyed by the  bombing and the deployment war you  mentioned Cambodia that was supposed to  be a neutral country was it not that  wasn't even supposed to be involved yet  America was fine secret missions in  there that was one of the things that  would lead to my decision to not  continue flying anymore I flew the State  Department in there on secret missions  and during that time I found out about  the secret bombing warf which had been  going on for more than a year I was led  to believe by intelligence operatives  who were friends and in Saigon that  after the government of sanik fell that  we had in fact engineered the coup that  led to lon all coming to power because  we wanted to bomb and invade the  sanctuary and CNO had said no that's  going to engulf us in this war and I  found myself flying troops to the border  that is a Cambodian Vietnamese  in late April of 1970 preparing for  invasion of Cambodia and that was the  time when I decided I didn't want to  have anything more to do with it and  what the hell deerforce react to this  well they didn't know quite how to react  because I had no contact with an  anti-war movement it wasn't a political  statement it was a statement of what I  perceived as right and wrong and I even  still felt obliged to stay in the Air  Force so I said I would like a change of  duty not related to Southeast Asia and  explained that I was becoming more and  more angry about everything I saw there  and felt that it would be better if I  had a change of assignment so I was sent  as a pilot was who who had any problems  was sent to a psychiatrist  that psychiatrist said he had to send me  back to the United States where someone  could evaluate me when I started talking  about a secret bombing war a secret  invasion a Phoenix program in which  thousands of suspected peasant  sympathizers were being assassinated I  think he thought I was crazier than a  lute and he committed me to a  psychiatric ward there in the ward I  went through a lot of confusion I went  through some depression some anger I  wasn't quite sure what I was doing there  and eventually got a good lawyer who  helped me to kind of make some decisions  and that was that I could have fought  this in the courts and he basically said  I had no chance at all that they would  throw the book at me I wasn't a  conscientious objector I refused to say  in fact I wouldn't have fought in a  non-combat capacity in World War two so  the choice was to accept a psychiatric  discharge which I which I did and what  year was this that was in 1971 when I  was finally discharged it not ironic  that sounds so much like what happens in  the Soviet Union they find people who  they don't like or people who descent  they say you're crazy and they send you  to a psychiatric hospital which is the  same as jail well to some extent I don't  do it as an abuse of psychiatry like the  Soviet Union but I was an officer had I  been enlisted man I probably would have  been in jail you just instead of in a  psychiatric ward and I was on a national  radio program the other day and someone  called up from a cracy media and said  now we want those listeners to know that  this man is crazy that he has  miss charge psychiatrist then called up  and said I discharged lots of pilots  because any of them that that protested  what was going on met with a similar  kind of disposition so when there were a  lot of so I suppose there were others I  didn't meet but a few but time to me it  seems like a criterion of rationality to  get out of the war because what they're  doing there is wrong because they're  killing because there's all these  problems with the whole Vietnam foreign  policy but it seems to me a rational  person would see in - well it was a  cacciatore because what they said to me  was that I had to be willing to fly  anyplace in the world at any time for  the Air Force if I was to be fit to fly  I said I would fly anyplace in the world  for the airforce any time except in  Vietnam so that made me not fit to fly  by their criteria and so there was kind  of a catch-22 involved in the in the  whole time so when you got discharged  what happened that well I found myself  and it was 25 without having any idea of  what I wanted in life I had always done  what was expected and what was right I  had been the good high school student I  had been the good cadet I was an  outstanding graduate I had been the good  graduate student the good pile at the  good officer and now what was I to be  the good and it forced me to re-evaluate  my whole value system I would travel in  a third world along and work in the  third world and come to understand  politically what was happening in  Southeast Asia and also what our role in  the world was to a greater extent and  would come to accept us to some extent  the fact that my psychiatric discharge  wasn't going to affect me in the least  and doing all I wanted to do but were  the third-world countries you were in  and what experience led to these  insights well I worked in as a ordinary  seaman on some banana boats I guess you  might you might  home and so I visited Central America  and that was the first time I came in  contact with some of the conditions  there I would drive a tractor and work  as a as a field hand and some islands in  the Pacific would work as a health  worker and in India and living close to  people in the third world I came to  understand a little bit more how they  perceive the United States but also how  our policies affected their lives to  some extent when particularly when  you're in Central America did you see  the indigenous poverty or suffering or  oppression that the people are  experiencing that would lead them to  seek revolutionary change do you have  some experiences of that order did that  come later I saw that but the close  understanding of that that would lead to  the revolution came came later because  certainly the suffering there was not  much different than the suffering in  India and yet in India it has in fact  it's probably worse there it hasn't yet  led to the kind of change that we're  seeing in Latin America so then you went  to medical school and became a public  health I got a master's degree in public  health as well as a car that's correct  and then after graduating where did you  hang your shingle well I went to Salinas  California where I began working with a  large indigenous population of farm  workers an