 Brothers banking in 1914 I made  Nicaragua safe for the National City  Bank boys I made Haiti safe for American  sugar interest in 1916 this kind of  attitude toward Central and South  America that is ours to even lose an  American colony yes it's not ours and  certainly these people should be able to  decide some things for themselves that  was this how does this changed you in  Vietnam you were of the nonviolent  persuasion you know that have you had  any changes within within you to see  where maybe violence can be justified  after being down El Salvador well I  don't think pacifism or a commitment to  non-violence means except naively that  that those are not realities in this  world I think the most serious pacifists  are those that would look to remove the  causes of war of which are corruption  brutality the kind of poverty that we  see in Central America  my understanding of non-violence was  what certainly changed a lot in El  Salvador and I was able to maintain my  commitment to it but I was throwing a  rifle one time when when we were  evacuating about 12 patients and it took  two men each to carry the bamboo poles  that the hammocks were slung from and  one of them threw me a rifle and I had  to ask myself was I going to use that  and and what would it mean to leave my  body beside the road or would I allow  those patients who were wounded to fall  into the hands of the government  soldiers who were often especially  brutal with wounded patients what what  would that serve I didn't have to answer  that question  at another time in a in a bass Christian  community one of the casinos wanted to  know more about what it meant to be a  Quaker they couldn't understand why  didn't carry an arm because certainly  the other physicians did there and I  tried to explain a little bit about  non-violence and and this man a very  reply they stopped me and he said now my  job on the Hacienda he pointed to it it  was just over the way he said had been  to feed the dogs and I had to give them  a large bowl of milk every morning and I  could never afford milk for my children  he said when the dogs were ill I would  have to take him to the veterinarian and  Suchi toto and he said I had never been  able to afford to take my children to  the doctor in SUCI toto and some of them  died for lack of medical care so he said  you Gringo's think of violence in terms  of what's done with a machete or a gun  but there's another kind of violence of  violence to the spirit and until you've  seen your children starve you can't  understand that kind of violence and it  was a very profound lesson I don't think  the people in also door have resorted  the violence as anything but a last  resort and many of them even did that  very reluctantly let me make a plug for  medical aid and I would say this to the  tender listening audience that the  Salvadoran people really make a  distinction between the American people  and the American government and then you  can help in that process because when  they see the daily gifts from the  American government bombs rockets  machine-gun bullets it's a message of  destruction and you can give them a  message of hope in the form of of  medical aid I work with an organization  that distributes medical aid in El  Salvador it's called a Salvadoran  medical relief fund
 

